  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "+cN0l/MwVw4SBoc+rHUxhSS98e4=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:44:09.613Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`@powerhousedao/builder-tools` is the toolkit behind the Powerhouse builder experience, and it is deliberately consumed through **subpath imports** rather than one barrel:\n\n```ts\nimport { \u2026 } from \"@powerhousedao/builder-tools/connect-studio\";\nimport { \u2026 } from \"@powerhousedao/builder-tools/connect-build\";\nimport { \u2026 } from \"@powerhousedao/builder-tools/document-model-editor\";\nimport { \u2026 } from \"@powerhousedao/builder-tools/editor-utils\";\nimport \"@powerhousedao/builder-tools/style.css\";\n```\n\nThe five modules map to five jobs: **Connect Studio** (managing and configuring connections), **Connect Build** (build utilities), the **Document Model Editor**, **Editor Utils** (common editor functionality), and **Connect Utils** (shared connection utilities).\n\nWhy the split is worth noticing rather than skimming: `connect-build` is build-time tooling and `document-model-editor` is a UI. A single entry point would drag one into a bundle that only wanted the other \u2014 so the subpaths are what let a runtime consumer take the editor pieces without the build machinery. The stylesheet is a separate import for the same reason.\n\nThis is also the package Vetra Studio's editing surface is assembled from, which is why the document-model editor lives here rather than in the design system.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/builder-tools/README.md` \u2014 Features; Usage (subpath imports and style import)\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:44:09.613Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:44:09.613Z"
    }
  },
